prompt.description:
A STRUCTURED JSON DOCUMENT (passed as a string), NOT a plain-text prompt — Ideogram 4.0 parses this JSON as a render spec. The more relationships you pin down (positions, colors, per-element styling), the more grounded the result. Plain natural language also works but gives up the layout/color/text control. Schema:
{
  "high_level_description": "one sentence summarizing setting, subjects, and mood",
  "style_description": {
    "aesthetics": "overall visual style/treatment",
    "lighting": "light source, direction, quality, color temperature",
    "photo": "camera/lens specifics, or \"\" if non-photographic",
    "medium": "photography | oil painting | 3D render | illustration | ...",
    "color_palette": ["#RRGGBB", "... up to 16 overall hex colors"]
  },
  "compositional_deconstruction": {
    "background": "only the environment behind/around the elements",
    "elements": [
      {
        "type": "obj",
        "bbox": [y_min, x_min, y_max, x_max],
        "desc": "identity, pose, location, size, key details",
        "color_palette": ["#RRGGBB", "... up to 5 per element"]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "the literal string to render in the image",
        "bbox": [y_min, x_min, y_max, x_max],
        "desc": "styling of the text (font feel, weight, treatment)",
        "color_palette": ["#RRGGBB"]
      }
    ]
  }
}
bbox is [y_min, x_min, y_max, x_max] in 0–1000 normalized coordinates, origin top-left (y down, x right). Use type "text" with a literal "text" field for any in-image copy, and a separate "desc" for its styling. List elements roughly background-to-foreground.
